fieldDocs.Reply Markup:
Telegram's `reply_markup`, exactly as the Bot API spells it — passed through verbatim (no wrapping, no typed shape here; a malformed keyboard surfaces as a loud Telegram error at run time). Usually an inline keyboard: rows of buttons, each `{ text, url }` (opens a link). To make a tap DO something, write a form from the `@lf/forms` adapter (`form = write forms-[:Forms]-> { Title: …, Fields: … }`), put its `form.Url` on a `url` button, and `await form-[:Submissions]->`: the form also asks for anything a person has to fill in, which Telegram does not capture at tap time. A `{ text, callback_data }` button reaches no run. Buttons compose with everything else on the send. Build strings with "${…}" interpolation (never `+`, which is numeric addition). Example:
{ inline_keyboard: [
  [ { text: "Answer", url: "${form.Url}" } ],
] }

